WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.560
We will evaluate the residue of
the full alternating sum, script S.

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:10.840
Let u be odd and greater than one, and
let I contain its distinct prime divisors.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:16.960
For each subset S, zeta sub S is the
product of its chosen prime roots.

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:22.840
The empty subset gives one. The sum
uses sign minus one to the size of S,

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:28.000
and the normalized value E at zeta
sub S divided by c at zeta sub S.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.320
Work in the same ring A, with i,

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:34.700
those roots, and one over
u adjoined to the integers.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:38.340
Node five gives a
coefficientwise congruence:

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:44.420
E of P is congruent to the all-ones
polynomial J of P modulo one plus i.

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:48.590
J contains every power from zero
through u squared minus one.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:51.200
Evaluation preserves that congruence.

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:54.780
At every subset input, E
and J have the same residue.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:58.980
We want to replace every numerator while
preserving its sign and denominator.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:03.220
The denominator step is
justified by units in A.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:08.640
The inverse of c at zeta sub S is its
complex conjugate divided by u squared.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:12.480
Multiplying c by that
inverse gives one exactly.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:16.680
Reduce this identity: the two
images still multiply to one.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.940
Thus the denominator stays
invertible after reduction.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:23.760
Multiply the congruence between
E and J by this inverse.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.620
The normalized values
have the same residue.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.700
This uses a unit of the evaluation ring,
not merely a nonzero complex value.

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:34.820
Make the replacement in
every term of script S.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:38.920
Node eight point one gives an exact zero:

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:44.160
J at zeta sub S equals zero
whenever S is nonempty.

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:49.020
Each corresponding whole term is
zero, since its denominator is a unit.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:53.440
Remove all those terms. Only
the empty subset remains.

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:57.580
Its sign is positive one
and its input is one.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:02.640
Therefore script S is congruent
to J of one divided by c of one.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:07.260
Node eight point two
evaluates this last quotient.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:13.460
J of one equals u squared, and c of
one equals positive or negative u.

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:18.320
Their quotient is positive or
negative u. Because u is odd,

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:22.300
both signs have residue
one modulo one plus i.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.700
The full alternating sum
therefore has residue one.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:29.520
See the same calculation
for u equal to fifteen.

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:35.920
The four subsets of three and five
have signs plus, minus, minus, plus.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:40.140
Their input orders are one,
three, five, and fifteen.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:45.480
The three nonempty-subset numerators
vanish exactly. The surviving

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:49.840
quotient is two hundred twenty-five
divided by positive or negative fifteen.

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:53.900
It equals positive or negative
fifteen, with residue one.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:03:00.020
The number of distinct prime divisors can
change, but exactly one term survives.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:05.020
Keep this residue one beside Node
seven's residue zero for the very

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:09.620
same sum in the very same quotient
ring. The next step compares them.
